# The Highland bagpipe's chanter: a conical double reed blown at a
# constant pressure from the bag, so it has no dynamics and no vibrato,
# and one timbre held at the peak is the instrument. Every harmonic is
# present, the fundamental under the 2nd to 5th: a bright, nasal tone,
# two formants near 1.3 kHz and 3 kHz across the chanter's octave (G4 to
# A5). Harmonic h at (R(f, 1300, 1.5) + 0.8 R(f, 3000, 2)) / (1 + (f /
# 6000)^2), f = h x 587.33 Hz (D5, the chanter's middle), R(f, c, q) =
# 1 / sqrt(1 + q^2 (f / c - c / f)^2), scaled to the loudest; the
# amplitudes at full precision. It speaks at once: under a slur the
# player only changes the step. At n 7 (28.6 kHz), not 10: at 20 kHz its
# strong 4-8 kHz harmonics put images at 12-16 kHz, 16 dB under the
# whole, heard as a high line moving against the tune; at 28.6 kHz they
# fall at 20-25 kHz (33 dB under in 12-16 kHz).
name bagpipe chanter
family pipes
